From: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] zebra: revive a MAC's neighbours when the MAC becomes local again

0126 stopped a kernel echo from clearing ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE and 0127
closed the same hole in the duplicated same-MAC fast path. Both are needed,
but together they leave the flag with no dependable way back: it is now only
cleared by a neighbour update that arrives while the neighbour's MAC is
already locally active.

That update is not guaranteed to come. With reachable-only advertisement
disabled, which is what SONiC runs, netlink_neigh_change() reports
local_inactive as false for every kernel update, so the MAC is the only
liveness signal left. A sync neighbour is programmed into the dataplane as a
static entry, which keeps the kernel's NUD state machine off it, so the
kernel has no reason to emit an update of its own once the host comes back.

zebra_evpn_add_update_local_mac() does not close the gap either. It does not
set upd_neigh on a plain local-inactive to local-active transition, so
zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_change() is never reached; and even
when it is reached it only clears the flag if the neighbour's state is
inactive or the sequence or ES changed, none of which is true here.

The result is a host that returns and has its MAC re-learned and
re-advertised as non-proxy, while its MAC-IP route stays a proxy route. The
ES peers run the peer-active hold timer to completion and retire the
neighbour of a host that is demonstrably back, taking ARP suppression and the
IP-to-MAC binding on the remote leaves with it.

Add the counterpart to the inactive walker and run it on the reverse
transition. It is skipped when reachable-only advertisement is enabled,
because there the neighbour's own NUD state is the liveness signal and the
MAC must not override it, and when upd_neigh is set, because that marks a MAC
that has just turned local or moved and the existing change walker already
revives the neighbours in both of those cases.

Like the inactive walker it is idempotent, and it leaves loc_seq alone: going
inactive did not change the sequence, so coming back should not either. Only
the proxy flag on the re-advertised MAC-IP route changes.

Signed-off-by: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
---
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c b/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
index 22150a3af0..dd76bce9a9 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
@@ -2356,8 +2356,17 @@ int zebra_evpn_add_update_local_mac(struct zebra_vrf *zvrf,
 	 * This is the path an ageing MAC actually arrives on: the dataplane
 	 * reports it as an update with local_inactive set, not as a delete.
 	 */
-	if (local_inactive && !old_local_inactive)
+	if (local_inactive && !old_local_inactive) {
 		zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(zevpn, mac);
+	} else if (!local_inactive && old_local_inactive && !upd_neigh) {
+		/* And the MAC brings its neighbours back when the host returns.
+		 * upd_neigh is excluded because it marks a MAC that has just
+		 * turned local or moved, and
+		 * zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_change() below already
+		 * revives the neighbours in both of those cases.
+		 */
+		zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_active(zevpn, mac);
+	}
 
 	/* Process all neighbors associated with this MAC, if required. */
 	if (upd_neigh)
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
index e033755ffb..cfc37559df 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
@@ -1097,6 +1097,60 @@ void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Process all neighbors associated with a local MAC that has just become
+ * locally active again.
+ *
+ * Counterpart to the inactive walker above. With reachable-only advertisement
+ * disabled the kernel reports local_inactive as false for every neighbour
+ * update, so that value carries no liveness information and a neighbour
+ * retired along with its MAC can only be revived by the MAC itself. A sync
+ * neighbour is programmed into the dataplane as a static entry, so the kernel
+ * has no reason to emit an update of its own once the host returns.
+ *
+ * Without this the neighbour stays locally inactive, its MAC-IP route stays a
+ * proxy route, and the ES peers run the hold timer to completion and retire a
+ * host that is demonstrably back.
+ */
+void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_active(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
+						  struct zebra_mac *zmac)
+{
+	struct zebra_neigh *n = NULL;
+	struct listnode *node = NULL;
+	bool old_bgp_ready;
+	bool new_bgp_ready;
+
+	/* When reachable-only advertisement is enabled the neighbour's own NUD
+	 * state is the liveness signal and the MAC must not override it.
+	 */
+	if (zebra_evpn_mh_do_adv_reachable_neigh_only())
+		return;
+
+	for (ALL_LIST_ELEMENTS_RO(zmac->neigh_list, node, n)) {
+		if (!CHECK_FLAG(n->flags, ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL))
+			continue;
+
+		if (!CHECK_FLAG(n->flags, ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE))
+			continue;
+
+		old_bgp_ready = zebra_evpn_neigh_is_ready_for_bgp(n);
+		UNSET_FLAG(n->flags, ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE);
+		new_bgp_ready = zebra_evpn_neigh_is_ready_for_bgp(n);
+
+		if (IS_ZEBRA_DEBUG_EVPN_MH_NEIGH)
+			zlog_debug("sync-neigh vni %u ip %pIA mac %pEA local-active on mac add f 0x%x",
+				   zevpn->vni, &n->ip, &n->emac, n->flags);
+
+		/* re-advertises the MAC-IP route without the proxy flag */
+		zebra_evpn_neigh_send_add_del_to_client(n, old_bgp_ready,
+							new_bgp_ready);
+
+		zebra_evpn_sync_neigh_dp_install(n, false /* set_inactive */,
+						 false /* force_clear_static */,
+						 __func__);
+	}
+}
+
 /*
  * Process all neighbors associated with a MAC upon the MAC being remotely
  * learnt.
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.h b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.h
index 370718e655..1c83f2187a 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.h
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.h
@@ -233,6 +233,8 @@ void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_del(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 					       struct zebra_mac *zmac);
 void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 						    struct zebra_mac *zmac);
+void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_active(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
+						  struct zebra_mac *zmac);
 void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_change(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 						  struct zebra_mac *zmac,
 						  bool seq_change,
